Research Report: Personal Knowledge and Habit Systems for Students
1. Target audience
College students and early-career developers who juggle classes, projects, internships and side learning, with limited time to maintain tools.
2. Research: knowledge management methods
PARA (Tiago Forte)
Organizes everything into four buckets: Projects (active, with a deadline), Areas (ongoing responsibilities such as health or coursework), Resources (topics of interest), and Archives (inactive items).
Strength: simple sorting rule; works well for task-driven people.
Weakness: needs regular upkeep to avoid clutter.
Zettelkasten (Niklas Luhmann method)
Small atomic notes, each with one idea, linked to other notes by IDs or tags. Knowledge grows through connections.
Strength: strong for long-term learning and writing.
Weakness: slower to start; can feel heavy for beginners.
Habit design
Useful ideas for keeping a system alive: make the first action tiny, attach it to an existing routine, and keep a weekly review as a fixed habit. Reducing friction matters more than having a perfect structure.
3. Research: AI tools used in this project
Notion: databases for tasks, goals and notes; good for structured tracking.
Obsidian: local Markdown notes with bidirectional links; good for linked learning notes.
Claude / ChatGPT / Gemini: summarizing notes, generating quizzes, drafting weekly reviews.
Mem (free tier): auto-organizes notes; used for comparison.
Gamma: quick slide generation for the presentation.
Zapier / Make: connects tools to automate one step.
